        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "The Electronics",
                    "id": "electronics",
                    "level": 2
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "For controlling the robot, I used stepper drivers and servo drivers controlled by an ESP32 microcontroller. To make the design semi-permanent, I used perfboard and a 3D-printed box to contain everything."
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "Power",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {

                    "type": "grid",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 0
                    },
                    "align": "start",
                    "elements": [
                        {
                            "type": "container",
                            "flex": "col",
                            "positioning": {
                                "marginTop": 0,
                                "marginInline": 0,
                                "alignSelf": "center"
                            },
                            "elements": [
                                {
                                    "type": "text",
                                    "content": "The robot arm is powered by an LRS-350-12 which, as its name implies, provides 350W at 12V. The steppers run on 12V, but for the servos, a buck converter brings it down to 6V. ",
                                    "positioning": {
                                        "marginInline": 0,
                                        "marginTop": 0
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "text",
                                    "content": "Another buck converter steps it down to 5V to power solely the ESP32. For communication, all parts share a ground plane.",
                                    "positioning": {
                                        "marginTop": 5,
                                        "marginInline": 0
                                    }
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            "type": "image",
                            "url": "/images/robot-arm/electronics.webp",
                            "alt": "Photo of the power supply and buck converters",
                            "title": "Electronics - Power",
                            "width": 100,
                            "height": 30,
                            "y": 50,
                            "screenSizes": {
                                "small": 100,
                                "medium": 80,
                                "large": 60
                            },
                            "positioning": {
                                "marginInline": 0,
                                "marginTop": 0
                            }
                        }

                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "Circuit",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {

                    "type": "grid",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "align": "start",
                    "elements": [
                        {
                            "type": "image",
                            "url": "/images/robot-arm/circuit_stack.webp",
                            "alt": "Photo of two perfboards stacked containing the control circuit",
                            "title": "Electronics - Circuit Stack",
                            "width": 100,
                            "height": 30,
                            "y": 50,
                            "screenSizes": {
                                "small": 100,
                                "medium": 80,
                                "large": 60
                            },
                            "positioning": {
                                "marginInline": 0,
                                "marginTop": 0
                            }
                        },
                        {
                            "type": "container",
                            "flex": "col",
                            "positioning": {
                                "marginTop": 5,
                                "marginInline": 0
                            },
                            "elements": [
                                {
                                    "type": "text",
                                    "content": "The electronics are two perfboards stacked on each other. On the top is a PCA9685, which controls the servos, and there are also bulk capacitors for the servos' high current spikes.",
                                    "positioning": {
                                        "marginInline": 0,
                                        "marginTop": 0
                                    }
                                },
                                {
                                    "type": "text",
                                    "content": "On the bottom layer are two TMC2209 drivers, which control the stepper motors, and the ESP32, which controls the entire robot.",
                                    "positioning": {
                                        "marginTop": 5,
                                        "marginInline": 0
                                    }
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "Enclosure",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "To help keep things together, I 3D printed a box to mount the circuit, power supply, and switch together.",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "url": "/images/robot-arm/electronics-case.webp",
                    "alt": "Photo of 3D printed box that holds the electronics",
                    "title": "Electronics - Case",
                    "screenSizes": {
                        "small": 100,
                        "medium": 70,
                        "large": 80
                    },
                    "width": 90,
                    "y": 50,
                    "positioning": {
