                    }
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "The Programming",
                    "id": "programming",
                    "level": 2
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "Finally, I made a locally-hosted webapp to control each motor on the arm. The program can be split into three parts: the ESP32s, the NodeJS backend, and the React dashboard."
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "ESP32s",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "There are two ESP32. One part of the control circuit (receiver), and another separately connected to a computer/server (sender). The sender ESP32 simply transmits what it receives over Serial to the receiver ESP32 over the ESP-NOW protocol.",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "For instance, to move the base stepper to **position 400** at **1ms intervals** with **1 step** per interval after a **5ms delay**, it would send: `{\"tgt\":\"base\",\"int\":1,\"del\":0,\"stp\":1}`",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "cpp",
                    "filename": "controller-receiver.ino",
                    "content": "int pos = 0;\nbool disable = doc.containsKey(\"disable\");\nbool enable = doc.containsKey(\"enable\");\nint interval = doc[\"int\"] | 5;\nint delay = doc[\"del\"] | 0;\nint steps = doc[\"stp\"] | 1;\n\nif (doc.containsKey(\"pos\")) {\n\tpos = doc[\"pos\"];\n} else if (!disable && !enable) {\n\tSerial.println(\"Error: No position provided\");\n\treturn;\n}\n\nconst char* targetId = doc[\"tgt\"];\nif (targetId == nullptr) {\n\tSerial.println(\"Error: Missing target key\");\n\treturn;\n}\n\n...\n\ntarget->setTarget(pos, interval, delay, steps);",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "NodeJS",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "The ESP32 receives the commands to send from a NodeJS that communicates directly through Serial over USB. The backend also hosts a websockets server and a database. This allows it to receive commands from a browswer dashboard, update other dashboards connected simultaneously, and store the motors' position and states.",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "For example, the dashboard might want to move base to **position 400**, so it'll send over websockets to the server: `{\"base\":400}`. The server will then populatet interval and steps automatically.",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "code",
                    "language": "javascript",
                    "filename": "server.js",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    },
                    "content": "const value = jsonData[key];\nif (typeof value === \"number\") {\n\tupdateDBValue.run(key, value);\n\tconsole.log(`  - Logged ${key} in database`);\n\n\tconst motor = MOTORS[key];\n\tconst jsonPayload = {\n\t\t\"tgt\": key,\n\t\t\"pos\": value,\n\t\t\"int\": motor[\"interval\"],\n\t\t\"stp\": motor[\"steps\"]\n\t}\n\n\tconst payload = JSON.stringify(jsonPayload);\n\tserialPrint(payload);\n}"
                },
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "Dashboard",
                    "level": 3,
                    "size": 2,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 20
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "The dashboard is built using React and compiled with Vite. It is a single page consisting of a slider, enable/disable toggle, and reset to starting position button. Because the dashboard is a webapp and keeps a connection to the backend, another device, like a phone, can be used as a controller simultaneously and both dashboards will update in realtime.",
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 5
                    }
                },
                {
                    "type": "image",
                    "url": "/images/robot-arm/dashboard.webp",
                    "alt": "Screenshot of the dashboard from Firefox",
                    "title": "Programming - Dashboard",
                    "screenSizes": {
                        "small": 100,
                        "medium": 80,
                        "large": 90
                    },
                    "width": 95,
                    "y": 50,
                    "positioning": {
                        "marginTop": 10
                    }
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "type": "section",
            "positioning": {
                "marginTop": 20
            },
            "elements": [
                {
                    "type": "header",
                    "content": "The Results",
                    "level": 2,
                    "id": "results"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "Below are two videos of the robot being controlled. Unfortunately, the quality isn't great because the robot was controlled offscreen by my phone, so this was recorded by my laptop."
                },
                {
                    "type": "ytvideo",
                    "videoId": "OYML8F32zfU"
                },
                {
                    "type": "ytvideo",
                    "videoId": "srlNwiMgDFg"
                },
                {
                    "type": "text",
                    "content": "Overall, I consider the project a success. There are things that could be improved, however. The overall design is bulkier than it needed to be, the same could've been accomplished with smaller parts. The steppers are pretty slow and because of how the belts are made the belt occasionally slips. The servo on the claw risks burning out when trying to grab things, a different type of servo should have been used or a different type of motor. These are just a few of the improvements that could be made, but despite these, I'm still happy that I was able to get something together."
                }
            ]
        }

    ]
}